# {{lb|en|of [[textile]]s}} Dyed before being formed into [[cloth]].
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|figuratively}} Firmly [[establish]]ed in a person's [[belief]]s or [[habit]]s; deeply [[ingrain]]ed in the [[nature]] of a person or thing.
#: {{ux|en|Smith was a '''dyed-in-the-wool''' typist and never really got used to writing on computers.}}
#: {{ux|en|John Major was described by his opponents as a '''dyed-in-the-wool''' Conservative.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 April 7|author=Christian Wolmar|title=Electrification is a given... but comfort matters as well|journal=RAIL|issue=928|page=47|text=Again, to go back to the history of British Railways, there were moves to introduce electrification more widely when the West Coast Main Line was sparked up in the 1960s, but this was rejected by '''dyed-in-the-wool''' old regional railway managers who did not like the hassle of putting up the wires.}}

====Usage notes====
The expression "'''dyed in the wool'''" refers to a state of steadfastness, especially with respect to one's [[political]], [[religious]] or social beliefs.

